1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,865
How big can an airport be?

2
00:00:01,865 --> 00:00:05,670
This is London Heathrow in comparison to Manhattan, normal enough.

3
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:11,500
This is Dubai's current international airport, a little bigger, but it still seems okay.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:17,825
But here is the new airport Dubai is building, Al Maktoum International, a place bigger than Manhattan.

5
00:00:17,825 --> 00:00:20,965
This is less an airport and more a city with some planes.

6
00:00:20,965 --> 00:00:25,460
It'll have the capacity for more than 260 million passengers a year.

7
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:32,265
There'll be five parallel runways, 400 aircraft gates, and it'll ferry some 12 million tons of cargo.

8
00:00:32,265 --> 00:00:35,990
Oh, and the price tag, 35 billion US dollars.

9
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:39,955
It's the most ambitious piece of transport infrastructure on the planet.

10
00:00:39,955 --> 00:00:47,210
Not only is it set to radically transform the aviation industry, but it could change this entire region forever.

11
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:49,080
Or at least that's the plan.

12
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:54,610
Now this airport is big, but we kind of need to really bring home for you just how big it really is.

13
00:00:54,610 --> 00:01:05,495
And before any of you run off to the comment section, yes, King Fahd and Denver airport are technically bigger than Al Maktoum will be, but they're both far smaller in terms of the space actually used.

14
00:01:05,495 --> 00:01:09,505
Geographically, sure, it's larger than the island of Manhattan.

15
00:01:09,505 --> 00:01:11,990
But what does that actually translate to?

16
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:18,930
London's Heathrow, one of the world's busiest international hubs, handles around 84 million passengers a year.

17
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:27,285
Hartsfield-Jackson in Atlanta, often ranked as the world's busiest airport overall, processes more than 100 million passengers a year.

18
00:01:27,285 --> 00:01:36,705
Ethiopia's Bishoftu airport, currently under construction, is looking to break current records by having a passenger capacity of 110 million.

19
00:01:36,705 --> 00:01:40,465
But Al Maktoum is being designed for 260 million.

20
00:01:40,465 --> 00:01:46,750
That means that Dubai's new airport could accommodate more than double the passenger volume of today's busiest airports.

21
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:55,895
If we were comparing that to say the size of the entire US population, it would be three out of every four Americans traveling through a single airport every year.

22
00:01:55,895 --> 00:01:57,900
That's a hell of a lot of people.

23
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:05,770
On top of those hundreds of millions of passengers, you've also got to factor in the cargo capacity and that is absolutely enormous.

24
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:08,660
Some 12 million tons by 2032.

25
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:13,305
Hong Kong International Airport currently handles the most cargo in the world.

26
00:02:13,305 --> 00:02:19,160
But that's less than half Dubai's projected capacity and sits at around 5 million tons annually.

27
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:24,440
Actually, let's just quickly list all the world records that this new airport is set to break.

28
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:30,500
It's going to be the largest airport by passenger capacity, by cargo and by runway capacity.

29
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,640
It'll have the largest terminal development and the largest footprint of any airport on the planet.

30
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:41,495
Someone better call Guinness, because they're going to need a whole section on this project in the next book.

